 {"class_id": 3135, "class": "go-link-cut-tree-dynamic-mst-preferred-path-invariants", "title": "go-link-cut-tree-dynamic-mst-preferred-path-invariants", "description": "go-link-cut-tree-dynamic-mst-preferred-path-invariants", "answer_id": 4870, "language": "go", "environment": "go1.26", "version": "1.26", "solution": "Done. I diagnosed the failure, implemented the fix, and verified it with a randomized differential suite. The full self-contained document is at `~/solution/SOLUTION.md`, and the working code + tests are in `~/solution/`.\n\n# Fully Dynamic MST with Link-Cut Trees: Null-Sentinel Collision and Preferred-Path Invariants\n\n## Root-cause analysis\n\n**The actual bug — null sentinel collides with graph vertex `0`.** A link-cut tree uses node `0` as the `null` sentinel. The natural-but-wrong setup allocates nodes `0..n` and maps graph vertex `v` to node `v`, making vertex `0` *the same node as null*. It silently corrupts after ≥2 operations: node `0` acquires real splay children while `isRoot(x)` still returns true for them, so rotations skip parent updates and the preferred-path decomposition desynchronizes. Symptoms: `PathMax(a,b)` returns an edge not on `a..b`, `Connected` is wrong after evert, and the forest accumulates cycles.\n\nReproduced on vertices `{0,1,2}` with edges `0-1 (w=5)`, `1-2 (w=1)`:\n\n```\nPathMax(0,1) = (5,id0)\nPathMax(1,2) = (5,id0)   <-- WRONG, should be (1,id1)\n```\n\n**Exact fix:** reserve node `0` exclusively for null; store vertex `v` at LCT node `v+1`. The `DynamicMST` layer applies the offset at every call boundary (`FindRoot(u+1)`, `Link(u+1, e.node)`, `PathMax(u+1, v+1)`, `Cut(e.U+1, ...)`, …).\n\nSecondary invariants also covered:\n- `best[x]` must be recomputed by `pushup` after every structural change; it is reversal-invariant, so it's safe across lazy evert.\n- `splay` must push lazy tags top-down before any rotation; `FindRoot` pushes before descending left.\n- Deterministic tie-breaking needs a total order `(weight, id)`: path max = largest; insert replaces only if `w < wmax || (w == wmax && id < idmax)`; replacement = smallest crossing edge.\n- Insertions use the cycle property, deletions the cut property.\n\n## Exact fix (core)\n\n```go\n// NewLCT reserves node 0 for null; graph vertex v lives at node v+1.\nfunc (d *DynamicMST) Insert(id, u, v int, w int64) {\n\te := &Edge{ID: id, U: u, V: v, W: w, node: d.L.AddNode(w, id)}\n\td.Edges[id] = e\n\tif d.L.FindRoot(u+1) != d.L.FindRoot(v+1) {   // different components\n\t\td.L.Link(u+1, e.node); d.L.Link(e.node, v+1); e.inTree = true; return\n\t}\n\t_, maxID, _ := d.L.PathMax(u+1, v+1)          // cycle property\n\tif maxID >= 0 {\n\t\tmax := d.Edges[maxID]\n\t\tif w < max.W || (w == max.W && id < max.ID) {\n\t\t\td.cutTreeEdge(max); d.NonTree[maxID] = max\n\t\t\td.L.Link(u+1, e.node); d.L.Link(e.node, v+1); e.inTree = true; return\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\td.NonTree[id] = e\n}\n\nfunc (d *DynamicMST) Delete(id int) (int, bool) {\n\te, ok := d.Edges[id]; if !ok { return 0, false }\n\tdelete(d.Edges, id)\n\tif !e.inTree { delete(d.NonTree, id); return 0, false }\n\td.cutTreeEdge(e)                              // cut property\n\tvar best *Edge\n\tfor _, f := range d.NonTree {\n\t\tif d.L.FindRoot(f.U+1) != d.L.FindRoot(f.V+1) {\n\t\t\tif best == nil || f.W < best.W || (f.W == best.W && f.ID < best.ID) { best = f }\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif best == nil { return 0, false }\n\tdelete(d.NonTree, best.ID)\n\td.L.Link(best.U+1, best.node); d.L.Link(best.node, best.V+1); best.inTree = true\n\treturn best.ID, true\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\n```bash\ncd ~/solution\ngofmt -l . && go vet ./... && go test -race -count=1 -v ./...\n```\n\n```\n--- PASS: TestRandomLCT             (raw LCT: Connected + PathMax vs brute, 500 seeds)\n--- PASS: TestRandomDynamicMST      (400 seeds × 400 ops vs Kruskal; cycles + splits)\n--- PASS: TestAllEqualWeights       (deterministic (weight,id) tie-break)\n--- PASS: TestDeletionSplitsComponent\n--- PASS: TestCycleInsertion\n--- PASS: TestLCTPathMax\nPASS\nok      dynmst  5.293s\n```\n\nThe Kruskal reference uses the identical total order, so the tests assert exact edge-set equality (not just equal total weight) after every mutation. All files are `gofmt`-clean and `go vet`-clean.\n\nOne honesty note on complexity: each LCT primitive is `O(log n)` amortized and insertion is `O(log n)`; the deletion replacement step scans the maintained non-tree set, so it is `O(k log n)` for `k` non-tree edges. The document notes that layering the Holm–de Lichtenberg–Thorup weight hierarchy on top makes replacement polylogarithmic without changing any of the invariants above.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: go-link-cut-tree-dynamic-mst-preferred-path-invariants\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-11-03T09:01:44.402Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Implement a fully dynamic minimum spanning tree over a stream of weighted edge insertions and deletions using link-cut trees with preferred-path bookkeeping: maintain path-maximum edge queries, splay strictness invariants under evert, and deterministic tie-breaking on equal weights. In O(log n) amortised time per operation, report the replacement edge (if any) when a tree edge is removed, and after every mutation assert that the maintained forest is still a minimum spanning forest, including insertions that create exactly one cycle and deletions that split a component into two.", "environment": "go1.26", "language": "go", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "go-link-cut-tree-dynamic-mst-preferred-path-invariants", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-11-03T09:01:44.402Z", "version": "1.26"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-11-03 09:01:44"}
 {"class_id": 3136, "class": "js-qpack-blocked-insert-dynamic-table-eviction", "title": "js-qpack-blocked-insert-dynamic-table-eviction", "description": "js-qpack-blocked-insert-dynamic-table-eviction", "answer_id": 4871, "language": "js", "environment": "node20", "version": "20", "solution": "Done. The complete, verified solution is in **`/workspace/SOLUTION.md`** (self-contained, 74 KB), with the implementation in **`/workspace/qpack.js`** and the suite in **`/workspace/test.js`**.\n\n## Title\nRFC 9204 QPACK Encoder/Decoder State Machines for a Simulated HTTP/3 Connection\n\n## Root-cause analysis (the traps a naive implementation hits)\n\n1. **Truncated Huffman table** — hand-built table had 228/257 symbols (Kraft sum 0.546 instead of 1), producing invalid codes. Fixed by deriving the table from RFC 7541 Appendix B; the known vector `\"www.example.com\" → f1e3c2e5f23a6ba0ab90f4ff` now matches.\n2. **Buffer/string type drift** — decoder stored dynamic-table *values* as `Buffer` while names were strings, breaking exact-match lookup. Fixed by normalizing at the single insertion point.\n3. **Relative vs post-base indexing** — `abs = Base-1-i` vs `abs = Base+i`. Both mappings are now explicit; the encoder defaults `Base = RIC` and accepts `{base}` to force post-base encoding.\n4. **Required Insert Count is a modulo code** — resolved relative to `totalInserts + MaxEntries`; only unambiguous within the live window `totalInserts-MaxEntries < RIC <= totalInserts`.\n5. **Eviction** — must evict to `capacity - newSize`, protect entries referenced by unacknowledged sections, and refuse a too-large entry (`QPACK_ENCODER_STREAM_ERROR`), not blindly clear as HPACK does.\n6. **Blocked sections** — a section with `RIC > inserts received` must be buffered, never decoded.\n7. **Three disjoint error domains** — field section → `QPACK_DECOMPRESSION_FAILED`, encoder stream → `QPACK_ENCODER_STREAM_ERROR`, decoder stream → `QPACK_DECODER_STREAM_ERROR`; stream cancellation must not be a decompression failure.\n\n## Exact fix\nThe full `qpack.js` implementation is embedded verbatim in `SOLUTION.md`. It exports `QpackEncoder`, `QpackDecoder`, `DynamicTable`, `huffmanEncode/Decode`, integer and RIC coders, and `QpackError`/`ERR`. Run:\n\n```bash\nnode --check qpack.js\nnode test.js        # 36 assertions, exit 0\n```\n\n## Verification\n`36/36 tests passed`. Coverage includes:\n- **RFC-normative byte replay**: decoder replays the exact RFC 9204 Appendix B bytes (`3fbd01`, `c00f…`, `0381 10 11`, duplicate `02`, `0500 80 c1 81`, eviction `810d…`) and checks table capacity, size, insert counts and decoded headers at each step.\n- **RFC 7541 vectors**: integer `1337 → 1f 9a 0a`, Huffman `www.example.com`.\n- **Header blocking** across interleaved streams (S2, S0, S1 delivered before inserts; per-stream `drainBlocked`).\n- **Eviction bounded by capacity** plus protection of live referenced entries until ack.\n- **never-indexed** and **literal-with-Huffman** field lines.\n- **Error taxonomy**, including the cancellation case that must *not* raise `QPACK_DECOMPRESSION_FAILED`.\n- **RIC guard**, asserting a section is never decoded before `RIC <= insertCount`.\n\nI also verified the document is genuinely self-contained: extracting its two `javascript` blocks into a clean directory and running `node test.js` reproduces `36/36 tests passed`.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: js-qpack-blocked-insert-dynamic-table-eviction\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-11-03T09:46:33.246Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Implement RFC 9204 QPACK encoder and decoder state machines driving a simulated HTTP/3 connection: dynamic table insert-with-name-reference, duplicate, and eviction bounded by Set Dynamic Table Capacity, plus header-blocking semantics driven by Required Insert Count across several interleaved request streams. The decoder must honour never-indexed and literal-with-Huffman field lines, distinguish QPACK_DECOMPRESSION_FAILED from QPACK_ENCODER_STREAM_ERROR on stream cancellation, and never process a field section whose Required Insert Count exceeds the highest acknowledged insert count advertised by the peer.", "environment": "node20", "language": "js", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "js-qpack-blocked-insert-dynamic-table-eviction", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-11-03T09:46:33.247Z", "version": "20"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-11-03 09:46:33"}
 {"class_id": 3137, "class": "python3-karlin-altschul-effective-length-bit-score-evalue", "title": "python3-karlin-altschul-effective-length-bit-score-evalue", "description": "python3-karlin-altschul-effective-length-bit-score-evalue", "answer_id": 4872, "language": "python3", "environment": "python3", "version": "3.13", "solution": "I diagnosed the problem by tracing every failure mode against the NCBI BLAST reference implementation (`blast_stat.c`, BLAST 2.17.0+), then wrote a self-contained Python module and verified it numerically. The complete solution is saved at `~/SOLUTION.md`; the runnable code is `~/karlin_altschul.py` and `~/verify_solution.py`. The markdown report follows.\n\n---\n\n# BLAST-style Karlin-Altschul statistics for gapless local alignments\n\n**Problem class:** `python3-karlin-altschul-effective-length-bit-score-evalue`\n**Deliverable:** `karlin_altschul.py` (self-contained, standard library only) plus a verification harness `verify_solution.py`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Root-cause analysis\n\nNaive implementations of ungapped Karlin-Altschul statistics fail in five distinct, reproducible ways. Each one has a precise fix, listed below.\n\n### 1.1 Solving `sum_ij p_i p_j exp(lambda s_ij) = 1` directly\n\nThe natural formulation works on `phi(lambda) = sum_s P(s) exp(lambda s) - 1`. `phi(0) = 0`, `phi'(0) = E[s] < 0`, and `phi(lambda) -> +inf`, so a unique positive root exists. But iterating on `exp(lambda s)` overflows for large scores and Newton's method easily walks to `lambda = 0` (the spurious root at `x = 1`) or overshoots into the overflow region.\n\n**Fix.** Substitute `x = exp(-lambda)`. The equation becomes a polynomial in `x` with a root in `[0, 1]`; the two mathematical roots are `x = 1` and the root we want. NCBI's `NlmKarlinLambdaNR` keeps an uncertainty bracket `[a, b]` with `f(a) > 0 > f(b)`, takes a Newton step only if it stays inside the bracket *and* decreases `|f|` *and* `f'(x) < 0`, and bisects otherwise. This is what `_lambda_newton` / `_calc_lambda` implement. Splitting out the greatest common divisor `d` of the attainable scores before calling prevents near-degenerate lattices.\n\n### 1.2 Silent NaN / `-1` on matrices that admit no solution\n\nWhen `E[s] >= 0` or no positive score occurs, `lambda` does not exist. Returning `-1`, `NaN`, or `inf` propagates into bit scores and E-values.\n\n**Fix.** Raise typed exceptions with a machine-readable `reason`:\n\n| condition | exception | `reason` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `E[s] >= 0` | `NegativeExpectedScoreError` | `nonnegative_expected_score` |\n| no positive score | `NoPositiveScoreError` | `no_positive_score` |\n| Newton/bisection cannot bracket a root | `LambdaNoSolutionError` | `lambda_underflow` |\n| `H <= 0` or `K` non-convergent | `KParameterError` | `k_parameter_undefined` |\n\nAll derive from `KarlinAltschulError`.\n\n### 1.3 A wrong (or approximate) `K`\n\n`K` is **not** `H/lambda` and it is **not** `exp(-2*outerSum) * something` that can be guessed. NCBI computes it with a dynamic program over the probability of each cumulative score for each alignment length (`P(i, j)`), followed by Horner evaluation, a truncated series in `outerSum`, and two closed forms for the special `{+1, -1}` and `{+1, x}` / `{x, -1}` systems. `H` itself is the relative entropy `lambda * sum_s s P(s) exp(lambda s)`.\n\n**Fix.** Port `BlastKarlinLHtoK` and `BlastKarlinLtoH` verbatim (`_LH_to_K`, `_calc_H`). The `divisor` (gcd) scaling, the `expm1` form of the denominator and the `sumlimit = 1e-4` / `iterlimit = 100` truncation are all load-bearing.\n\n### 1.4 Effective length without the composition / edge-effect term\n\nThe effective search space is `(m - A)(n - N*A)`, where the length adjustment `A` is the fixed point of\n\n```\nA = (alpha/lambda) * (ln K + ln((m - A)(n - N A))) + beta\n```\n\nFor ungapped alignments `alpha/lambda = 1/H` and `beta` is the tabulated edge-effect decay term (e.g. `-3.2` for BLOSUM62). Dropping `beta`, or using the raw sequence lengths, changes E-values by orders of magnitude on short sequences. `A` must be the *floor* of the fixed point, with the `ceil(A)` re-check that NCBI performs.\n\n**Fix.** Port `BLAST_ComputeLengthAdjustment` (`compute_length_adjustment`) and expose `effective_lengths(...)`.\n\n### 1.5 Rounding and multiple-HSP statistics\n\nBit scores are printed as half-up integers (`floor(x + 0.5)`), and multiple linked HSPs use the Karlin-Altschul 1993 sum statistics: the normalised total\n\n```\nxsum = sum_i (lambda*S_i - ln K)\n```\n\nis corrected for the pair search space and the number of starting points, then fed to the sum P-value (closed-form/interpolated for `r <= 4`, the double integral of K&A 1993 otherwise). A plain Poisson or a sum of rounded bit scores is wrong.\n\n**Fix.** `round_bit_score`, `sum_pvalue` and `sum_statistics`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Exact fix\n\nThe complete, dependency-free implementation is below. It is also saved as `~/karlin_altschul.py`.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nBLAST-style Karlin-Altschul statistics for gapless (ungapped) local alignments.\n\nThis module derives the ungapped parameters (lambda, K, H) for an arbitrary\ninteger substitution matrix and background residue frequencies by solving\n\n        sum_{i,j} p_i q_j exp(lambda * s_ij) = 1\n\nwith a safeguarded, bracketed Newton iteration on the polynomial in x = exp(-lambda).\nIt then provides the composition-based effective-length adjustment\n(edge-effect decay term beta included), bit scores, expectation values and\nmultiple-HSP sum statistics.\n\nThe numerical algorithms are a faithful port of the NCBI BLAST implementation\n(blast_stat.c: NlmKarlinLambdaNR / BlastKarlinLtoH / BlastKarlinLHtoK /\nBLAST_ComputeLengthAdjustment / s_BlastSumP) so results reproduce the\nreference tabulations.\n\nAll failure modes raise a typed KarlinAltschulError subclass instead of\nreturning NaN/-1 silently.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport math\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Callable, Dict, Iterable, List, Optional, Sequence, Tuple\n\n__all__ = [\n    \"KarlinAltschulError\",\n    \"NegativeExpectedScoreError\",\n    \"NoPositiveScoreError\",\n    \"LambdaNoSolutionError\",\n    \"KParameterError\",\n    \"KarlinStats\",\n    \"EffectiveLengths\",\n    \"ungapped_karlin\",\n    \"ungapped_karlin_from_scores\",\n    \"score_probabilities\",\n    \"compute_length_adjustment\",\n    \"effective_lengths\",\n    \"bit_score\",\n    \"round_bit_score\",\n    \"evalue\",\n    \"sum_statistics\",\n]\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Typed failures\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\nclass KarlinAltschulError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Base class for all Karlin-Altschul calculation failures.\"\"\"\n\n    reason = \"karlin_altschul_error\"\n\n\nclass NegativeExpectedScoreError(KarlinAltschulError):\n    \"\"\"The background expected score is >= 0, so lambda does not exist.\"\"\"\n\n    reason = \"nonnegative_expected_score\"\n\n\nclass NoPositiveScoreError(KarlinAltschulError):\n    \"\"\"No strictly positive score occurs with non-zero probability.\"\"\"\n\n    reason = \"no_positive_score\"\n\n\nclass LambdaNoSolutionError(KarlinAltschulError):\n    \"\"\"Newton/bisection failed to bracket a positive root (e.g. underflow).\"\"\"\n\n    reason = \"lambda_underflow\"\n\n\nclass KParameterError(KarlinAltschulError):\n    \"\"\"K (or H) could not be computed for the scoring system.\"\"\"\n\n    reason = \"k_parameter_undefined\"\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Result types\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass KarlinStats:\n    \"\"\"Ungapped Karlin-Altschul parameters.\"\"\"\n\n    Lambda: float\n    K: float\n    H: float\n    logK: float\n    score_avg: float\n    obs_min: int\n    obs_max: int\n\n    # convenience aliases matching the BLAST field names\n    @property\n    def lam(self) -> float:\n        return self.Lambda\n\n    @property\n    def k(self) -> float:\n        return self.K\n\n    @property\n    def h(self) -> float:\n        return self.H\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass EffectiveLengths:\n    \"\"\"Composition-corrected lengths and search space.\"\"\"\n\n    length_adjustment: int\n    query_effective_length: int\n    database_effective_length: int\n    search_space: float\n    converged: bool\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Core: build the score probability distribution\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\ndef score_probabilities(\n    matrix,\n    query_freq: Dict[str, float],\n    db_freq: Optional[Dict[str, float]] = None,\n) -> Dict[int, float]:\n    \"\"\"Return P(score = s) for all integer scores s.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    matrix:\n        Either a mapping ``(a, b) -> int score`` or a pair\n        ``(alphabet, rows)`` where ``rows`` is a square list-of-lists of ints.\n    query_freq, db_freq:\n        Background residue frequencies keyed by residue letter.  If ``db_freq``\n        is omitted the query frequencies are used for both sequences\n        (standard BLAST behaviour).\n    \"\"\"\n    if db_freq is None:\n        db_freq = query_freq\n\n    if isinstance(matrix, tuple):\n        alphabet, rows = matrix\n        mat = {(a, b): rows[i][j] for i, a in enumerate(alphabet)\n               for j, b in enumerate(alphabet)}\n    else:\n        mat = matrix\n\n    # normalise frequencies defensively\n    q_tot = sum(query_freq.values())\n    d_tot = sum(db_freq.values())\n    if q_tot <= 0 or d_tot <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"residue frequencies must be positive\")\n\n    sprob: Dict[int, float] = {}\n    for (a, b), s in mat.items():\n        pa = query_freq.get(a, 0.0) / q_tot\n        pb = db_freq.get(b, 0.0) / d_tot\n        if pa == 0.0 or pb == 0.0:\n            continue\n        sprob[s] = sprob.get(s, 0.0) + pa * pb\n    return sprob\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Core: lambda\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\ndef _gcd(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    a, b = abs(a), abs(b)\n    while b:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    return a\n\n\ndef _lambda_newton(\n    sprob: Dict[int, float],\n    d: int,\n    low: int,\n    high: int,\n    lambda0: float,\n    tolx: float,\n    itmax: int,\n    max_newton: int,\n) -> Tuple[float, int]:\n    \"\"\"Safeguarded Newton iteration on f(x) = sum p_s x**(s/d) - 1.\n\n    A root is bracketed in [a, b] subset [0, 1] with f(a) > 0 and f(b) < 0.\n    Newton steps are only accepted when they stay inside the bracket and\n    decrease |f|; otherwise the algorithm bisects.  This cannot converge to\n    x = 1 (lambda = 0) nor overflow, because it iterates on the polynomial in\n    x = exp(-lambda) rather than on the exponential directly.\n    \"\"\"\n    x0 = math.exp(-lambda0)\n    x = x0 if 0.0 < x0 < 1.0 else 0.5\n    a, b = 0.0, 1.0\n    f = 4.0  # larger than any possible value of the polynomial on [0, 1]\n    is_newton = 0\n    k = 0\n    for k in range(itmax):\n        fold = f\n        was_newton = is_newton\n        is_newton = 0\n\n        # Horner evaluation of f and f'\n        g = 0.0\n        f = sprob.get(low, 0.0)\n        i = low + d\n        while i < 0:\n            g = x * g + f\n            f = f * x + sprob.get(i, 0.0)\n            i += d\n        g = x * g + f\n        f = f * x + sprob.get(0, 0.0) - 1.0\n        i = d\n        while i <= high:\n            g = x * g + f\n            f = f * x + sprob.get(i, 0.0)\n            i += d\n\n        if f > 0.0:\n            a = x\n        elif f < 0.0:\n            b = x\n        else:\n            break  # exact root\n\n        if b - a < 2.0 * a * (1.0 - b) * tolx:\n            x = (a + b) / 2.0\n            break\n\n        if (\n            k >= max_newton\n            or (was_newton and abs(f) > 0.9 * abs(fold))\n            or g >= 0.0\n        ):\n            x = (a + b) / 2.0\n        else:\n            p = -f / g\n            y = x + p\n            if y <= a or y >= b:\n                x = (a + b) / 2.0\n            else:\n                is_newton = 1\n                x = y\n                if abs(p) < tolx * x * (1.0 - x):\n                    break\n\n    return -math.log(x) / d, k\n\n\ndef _calc_lambda(sprob: Dict[int, float], low: int, high: int,\n                 lambda0: float = 0.5) -> float:\n    score_avg = sum(s * p for s, p in sprob.items())\n    if high <= 0:\n        raise NoPositiveScoreError(\n            \"the scoring system has no positive score: lambda does not exist\"\n        )\n    if score_avg >= 0.0:\n        raise NegativeExpectedScoreError(\n            f\"expected score {score_avg:.6g} is not negative: lambda does not exist\"\n        )\n\n    # greatest common divisor of the lattice of attainable scores\n    d = -low\n    for i in range(1, high - low + 1):\n        if d <= 1:\n            break\n        if sprob.get(low + i, 0.0) != 0.0:\n            d = _gcd(d, i)\n\n    lam, _ = _lambda_newton(sprob, d, low, high, lambda0,\n                            tolx=1e-6, itmax=20, max_newton=20)\n    if not math.isfinite(lam) or lam <= 0.0:\n        raise LambdaNoSolutionError(\n            \"failed to bracket a positive root for lambda (numerical underflow)\"\n        )\n    return lam\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Core: H and K\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\ndef _calc_H(sprob: Dict[int, float], low: int, high: int, lam: float) -> float:\n    etonlam = math.exp(-lam)\n    s = low * sprob.get(low, 0.0)\n    for score in range(low + 1, high + 1):\n        s = score * sprob.get(score, 0.0) + etonlam * s\n    scale = etonlam ** high\n    if scale > 0.0:\n        return lam * s / scale\n    # underflow of exp(-lambda*high)\n    if s <= 0.0:\n        raise KParameterError(\"relative entropy H is not positive\")\n    return lam * math.exp(lam * high + math.log(s))\n\n\ndef _LH_to_K(sprob: Dict[int, float], low0: int, high0: int,\n             score_avg: float, lam: float, H: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Port of NCBI BlastKarlinLHtoK (PNAS 87, 2264-2268, appendix).\"\"\"\n    if lam <= 0.0 or H <= 0.0:\n        raise KParameterError(\"lambda and H must be positive to compute K\")\n    if score_avg >= 0.0:\n        raise NegativeExpectedScoreError(\"expected score must be negative\")\n\n    low, high = low0, high0\n    rng = high - low\n\n    divisor = -low\n    for i in range(1, rng + 1):\n        if divisor <= 1:\n            break\n        if sprob.get(low + i, 0.0) != 0.0:\n            divisor = _gcd(divisor, i)\n\n    high //= divisor\n    low //= divisor\n    lam *= divisor\n    rng = high - low\n\n    first_term = H / lam\n    exp_minus_lam = math.exp(-lam)\n\n    # closed forms for the special two- and three-point systems\n    if low == -1 and high == 1:\n        p_low = sprob[low0]\n        p_high = sprob[high0]\n        if p_low <= 0.0:\n            raise KParameterError(\"degenerate score distribution\")\n        return (p_low - p_high) ** 2 / p_low\n\n    if low == -1 or high == 1:\n        if high != 1:\n            score_avg = score_avg / divisor\n            first_term = (score_avg * score_avg) / first_term\n        return first_term * (1.0 - exp_minus_lam)\n\n    sumlimit = 1e-4\n    iterlimit = 100\n    asp = [0.0] * (iterlimit * rng + 1)  # P(total score i in j letters)\n\n    outer_sum = 0.0\n    low_as = high_as = 0\n    asp[0] = inner = oldsum = oldsum2 = 1.0\n    iter_counter = 0\n\n    while iter_counter < iterlimit and inner > sumlimit:\n        first = last = rng\n        low_as += low\n        high_as += high\n\n        # dynamic program: convolution over one more alignment position\n        idx = high_as - low_as\n        while idx >= 0:\n            ptr1 = idx - first\n            ptr1e = idx - last\n            ptr2 = first\n            inner = 0.0\n            while ptr1 >= ptr1e:\n                inner += asp[ptr1] * sprob.get(low + ptr2, 0.0)\n                ptr1 -= 1\n                ptr2 += 1\n            if first:\n                first -= 1\n            if idx <= rng:\n                last -= 1\n            asp[idx] = inner\n            idx -= 1\n\n        # Horner's rule over score classes (negative scores weighted by\n        # exp(-lambda), non-negative ones added directly)\n        ptrp = 0\n        inner = asp[ptrp]\n        i = low_as + 1\n        while i < 0:\n            ptrp += 1\n            inner = asp[ptrp] + inner * exp_minus_lam\n            i += 1\n        inner *= exp_minus_lam\n        while i <= high_as:\n            ptrp += 1\n            inner += asp[ptrp]\n            i += 1\n\n        oldsum2 = oldsum\n        oldsum = inner\n\n        iter_counter += 1\n        inner /= iter_counter\n        outer_sum += inner\n\n    K = -math.exp(-2.0 * outer_sum) / (first_term * math.expm1(-lam))\n    if not math.isfinite(K) or K <= 0.0:\n        raise KParameterError(\"K computation did not converge to a positive value\")\n    return K\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Public: full ungapped calibration\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\ndef ungapped_karlin_from_scores(\n    sprob: Dict[int, float],\n    lambda0: float = 0.5,\n) -> KarlinStats:\n    \"\"\"Compute (lambda, K, H) from a score probability distribution.\"\"\"\n    if not sprob:\n        raise NoPositiveScoreError(\"empty score distribution\")\n    low = min(sprob)\n    high = max(sprob)\n    score_avg = sum(s * p for s, p in sprob.items())\n\n    lam = _calc_lambda(sprob, low, high, lambda0)\n    H = _calc_H(sprob, low, high, lam)\n    if not math.isfinite(H) or H <= 0.0:\n        raise KParameterError(\"relative entropy H is not positive\")\n    K = _LH_to_K(sprob, low, high, score_avg, lam, H)\n    return KarlinStats(Lambda=lam, K=K, H=H, logK=math.log(K),\n                       score_avg=score_avg, obs_min=low, obs_max=high)\n\n\ndef ungapped_karlin(\n    matrix,\n    query_freq: Dict[str, float],\n    db_freq: Optional[Dict[str, float]] = None,\n    lambda0: float = 0.5,\n) -> KarlinStats:\n    \"\"\"Compute the ungapped Karlin-Altschul parameters for a matrix/frequencies.\"\"\"\n    sprob = score_probabilities(matrix, query_freq, db_freq)\n    return ungapped_karlin_from_scores(sprob, lambda0)\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Composition-based effective length adjustment\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\ndef compute_length_adjustment(\n    K: float,\n    logK: float,\n    alpha_over_lambda: float,\n    beta: float,\n    query_length: int,\n    db_length: int,\n    db_num_seqs: int,\n) -> Tuple[int, bool]:\n    \"\"\"Port of NCBI ``BLAST_ComputeLengthAdjustment``.\n\n    Returns ``(length_adjustment, converged)``.  ``alpha_over_lambda`` is\n    ``1/H`` for ungapped alignments and ``beta`` is the edge-effect decay\n    term (0 when unknown, negative values for the standard matrices).\n    \"\"\"\n    m = float(query_length)\n    n = float(db_length)\n    N = float(db_num_seqs)\n    max_iterations = 20\n\n    # Largest ell satisfying K (m - ell)(n - N ell) > max(m, n)\n    a = N\n    mb = m * N + n\n    c = n * m - max(m, n) / K\n    if c < 0.0:\n        return 0, True\n    disc = mb * mb - 4.0 * a * c\n    if disc < 0.0:\n        return 0, False\n    ell_max = 2.0 * c / (mb + math.sqrt(disc))\n\n    ell_min = 0.0\n    ell_next = 0.0\n    converged = False\n    for i in range(1, max_iterations + 1):\n        ell = ell_next\n        ss = (m - ell) * (n - N * ell)\n        if ss <= 0.0:\n            break\n        ell_bar = alpha_over_lambda * (logK + math.log(ss)) + beta\n        if ell_bar >= ell:\n            ell_min = ell\n            if ell_bar - ell_min <= 1.0:\n                converged = True\n                break\n            if ell_min == ell_max:\n                break\n        else:\n            ell_max = ell\n        if ell_min <= ell_bar <= ell_max:\n            ell_next = ell_bar\n        else:\n            ell_next = ell_max if i == 1 else (ell_min + ell_max) / 2.0\n\n    if converged:\n        length_adjustment = int(math.floor(ell_min))\n        # verify ceil(ell_min) does not share the floor of the true fixed point\n        ell = math.ceil(ell_min)\n        if ell <= ell_max:\n            ss = (m - ell) * (n - N * ell)\n            if ss > 0.0 and alpha_over_lambda * (logK + math.log(ss)) + beta >= ell:\n                length_adjustment = int(ell)\n    else:\n        length_adjustment = int(math.floor(ell_min))\n    return length_adjustment, converged\n\n\ndef effective_lengths(\n    stats: KarlinStats,\n    query_length: int,\n    db_length: int,\n    db_num_seqs: int = 1,\n    beta: float = 0.0,\n    alpha_over_lambda: Optional[float] = None,\n) -> EffectiveLengths:\n    \"\"\"Composition-based effective lengths and search space.\n\n    ``alpha_over_lambda`` defaults to ``1/H`` (the ungapped value).  ``beta`` is\n    the edge-effect decay term; pass the tabulated value (e.g. -3.2 for\n    BLOSUM62) to reproduce BLAST exactly.\n    \"\"\"\n    if alpha_over_lambda is None:\n        if stats.H <= 0.0:\n            raise KParameterError(\"H must be positive to derive alpha/lambda\")\n        alpha_over_lambda = 1.0 / stats.H\n\n    A, converged = compute_length_adjustment(\n        stats.K, stats.logK, alpha_over_lambda, beta,\n        query_length, db_length, db_num_seqs,\n    )\n    q_eff = max(query_length - A, 1)\n    db_eff = max(db_length - db_num_seqs * A, 1)\n    return EffectiveLengths(\n        length_adjustment=A,\n        query_effective_length=q_eff,\n        database_effective_length=db_eff,\n        search_space=float(q_eff) * float(db_eff),\n        converged=converged,\n    )\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Bit scores and E-values\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\ndef bit_score(score: float, stats: KarlinStats) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Unrounded BLAST bit score: (lambda*S - ln K)/ln 2.\"\"\"\n    return (score * stats.Lambda - stats.logK) / math.log(2.0)\n\n\ndef round_bit_score(score: float, stats: KarlinStats) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Bit score rounded half-up, matching BLAST tabular output.\"\"\"\n    bs = bit_score(score, stats)\n    return int(math.floor(bs + 0.5))\n\n\ndef evalue(score: float, stats: KarlinStats, search_space: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Expectation value E = searchspace * exp(-lambda*S + ln K).\"\"\"\n    return search_space * math.exp(-stats.Lambda * score + stats.logK)\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\n# Multiple-HSP sum statistics (S-prime)\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\ndef _ln_gamma_int(n: int) -> float:\n    return math.lgamma(float(n))\n\n\n# Tables interpolated by s_BlastSumP for r == 2, 3, 4\n_K_TAB2 = [\n    0.01669, 0.0249, 0.03683, 0.05390, 0.07794, 0.1111, 0.1559, 0.2146,\n    0.2890, 0.3794, 0.4836, 0.5965, 0.7092, 0.8114, 0.8931, 0.9490,\n    0.9806, 0.9944, 0.9989,\n]\n_K_TAB3 = [\n    0.9806, 0.9944, 0.9989, 0.0001682, 0.0002542, 0.0003829, 0.0005745,\n    0.0008587, 0.001278, 0.001893, 0.002789, 0.004088, 0.005958, 0.008627,\n    0.01240, 0.01770, 0.02505, 0.03514, 0.04880, 0.06704, 0.09103, 0.1220,\n    0.1612, 0.2097, 0.2682, 0.3368, 0.4145, 0.4994, 0.5881, 0.6765,\n    0.7596, 0.8326, 0.8922, 0.9367, 0.9667, 0.9846, 0.9939, 0.9980,\n]\n_K_TAB4 = [\n    2.658e-07, 4.064e-07, 6.203e-07, 9.450e-07, 1.437e-06, 2.181e-06,\n    3.302e-06, 4.990e-06, 7.524e-06, 1.132e-05, 1.698e-05, 2.541e-05,\n    3.791e-05, 5.641e-05, 8.368e-05, 0.0001237, 0.0001823, 0.0002677,\n    0.0003915, 0.0005704, 0.0008275, 0.001195, 0.001718, 0.002457,\n    0.003494, 0.004942, 0.006948, 0.009702, 0.01346, 0.01853, 0.02532,\n    0.03431, 0.04607, 0.06128, 0.08068, 0.1051, 0.1352, 0.1719, 0.2157,\n    0.2669, 0.3254, 0.3906, 0.4612, 0.5355, 0.6110, 0.6849, 0.7544,\n    0.8168, 0.8699, 0.9127, 0.9451, 0.9679, 0.9827, 0.9915, 0.9963,\n]\n\n_MAX_DIAGS = 20\n\n\ndef _romberg(\n    f: Callable[[float], float],\n    p: float,\n    q: float,\n    eps: float,\n    epsit: int,\n    itmin: int,\n) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Port of NCBI BLAST_RombergIntegrate.\"\"\"\n    itmin = max(1, min(itmin, _MAX_DIAGS - 1))\n    epsit = max(1, min(epsit, 3))\n    epsck = itmin - epsit\n\n    npts = 1\n    h = q - p\n    x = f(p)\n    if abs(x) == math.inf:\n        return x\n    y = f(q)\n    if abs(y) == math.inf:\n        return y\n    romb = [0.0] * _MAX_DIAGS\n    romb[0] = 0.5 * h * (x + y)\n    epsit_cnt = 0\n    for i in range(1, _MAX_DIAGS):\n        s = 0.0\n        x = p + 0.5 * h\n        for _ in range(npts):\n            y = f(x)\n            if abs(y) == math.inf:\n                return y\n            s += y\n            x += h\n        romb[i] = 0.5 * (romb[i - 1] + h * s)\n        n = 4\n        for j in range(i - 1, -1, -1):\n            romb[j] = (n * romb[j + 1] - romb[j]) / (n - 1)\n            n *= 4\n        if i > epsck:\n            if abs(romb[1] - romb[0]) > eps * abs(romb[0]):\n                epsit_cnt = 0\n            else:\n                epsit_cnt += 1\n                if i >= itmin and epsit_cnt >= epsit:\n                    return romb[0]\n        npts *= 2\n        h *= 0.5\n    return math.inf\n\n\ndef _sump_calc(r: int, s: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Port of NCBI s_BlastSumPCalc (double integral of K&A 1993, eqn 4).\"\"\"\n    if r == 1:\n        if s > 8.0:\n            return math.exp(-s)\n        return -math.expm1(-math.exp(-s))\n    if r < 1:\n        return 0.0\n\n    if r < 8:\n        if s <= -2.3 * r:\n            return 1.0\n    elif r < 15:\n        if s <= -2.5 * r:\n            return 1.0\n    elif r < 27:\n        if s <= -3.0 * r:\n            return 1.0\n    elif r < 51:\n        if s <= -3.4 * r:\n            return 1.0\n    elif r < 101:\n        if s <= -4.0 * r:\n            return 1.0\n\n    stddev = math.sqrt(r)\n    stddev4 = 4.0 * stddev\n    r1 = r - 1\n\n    if r > 100:\n        est_mean = -r * r1\n        if s <= est_mean - stddev4:\n            return 1.0\n\n    logr = math.log(r)\n    mean = r * (1.0 - logr) - 0.5\n    if s <= mean - stddev4:\n        return 1.0\n\n    if s >= mean:\n        t = s + 6.0 * stddev\n        itmin = 1\n    else:\n        t = mean + 6.0 * stddev\n        itmin = 2\n\n    num = r\n    num_minus_2 = r - 2\n    adj1 = num_minus_2 * logr - _ln_gamma_int(r1) - _ln_gamma_int(r)\n    epsilon = 0.002\n\n    def make_outer(inner_s: float):\n        sdvir = inner_s / num\n        adj2 = adj1 - inner_s\n\n        def outer(x: float) -> float:\n            y = math.exp(x - sdvir)\n            if y == math.inf:\n                return 0.0\n            if num_minus_2 == 0:\n                return math.exp(adj2 - y)\n            if x == 0.0:\n                return 0.0\n            return math.exp(num_minus_2 * math.log(x) + adj2 - y)\n\n        return outer\n\n    def inner_cb(inner_s: float) -> float:\n        mx = (inner_s / num + 3.0) if inner_s > 0.0 else 3.0\n        return _romberg(make_outer(inner_s), 0.0, mx, epsilon, 0, 1)\n\n    d = 0.0\n    while True:\n        d = _romberg(inner_cb, s, t, epsilon, 0, itmin)\n        if d == math.inf:\n            return d\n        if not (s < mean and d < 0.4 and itmin < 4):\n            break\n        itmin += 1\n    return d if d < 1.0 else 1.0\n\n\ndef sum_pvalue(r: int, s: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"P-value for r distinct alignments with adjusted total score ``s`` nats.\n\n    Exact port of NCBI ``s_BlastSumP`` (interpolated tables for r <= 4,\n    numerical integration otherwise).\n    \"\"\"\n    tables = {2: (_K_TAB2, len(_K_TAB2) - 1),\n              3: (_K_TAB3, len(_K_TAB3) - 1),\n              4: (_K_TAB4, len(_K_TAB4) - 1)}\n    if r == 1:\n        return -math.expm1(-math.exp(-s))\n    if r <= 4:\n        if r < 1:\n            return 0.0\n        r1 = r - 1\n        if s >= r * r + r1:\n            a = _ln_gamma_int(r + 1)\n            return r * math.exp(r1 * math.log(s) - s - a - a)\n        if s > -2 * r:\n            tab, size = tables[r]\n            a = s + s + 4 * r\n            i = int(a)\n            a -= i\n            i = size - i\n            return a * tab[i - 1] + (1.0 - a) * tab[i]\n        return 1.0\n    return _sump_calc(r, s)\n\n\ndef sum_statistics(\n    scores: Sequence[float],\n    stats: KarlinStats,\n    query_effective_length: int,\n    database_effective_length: int,\n    search_space: float,\n    starting_points: int,\n    round_scores: bool = False,\n) -> Dict[str, float]:\n    \"\"\"Multiple-HSP sum statistics for a linked set of HSPs.\n\n    Returns a dict with\n\n    * ``S_prime``        -- sum of the (optionally rounded) raw scores\n    * ``sum_bit_score``  -- S' expressed on the bit-scale\n    * ``xsum``           -- sum_i (lambda*S_i - ln K), the normalised total\n    * ``p_value``        -- small-gap sum p-value\n    * ``evalue``         -- small-gap sum expectation value\n    * ``num_hsps``       -- number of HSPs in the set\n    \"\"\"\n    if not scores:\n        raise ValueError(\"at least one HSP score is required\")\n    if starting_points < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"starting_points must be >= 1\")\n\n    used = [float(round(s)) if round_scores else float(s) for s in scores]\n    num = len(used)\n    s_prime = sum(used)\n    xsum = sum(stats.Lambda * s - stats.logK for s in used)\n\n    pair_search_space = float(database_effective_length) * float(query_effective_length)\n    if num == 1:\n        p = -math.expm1(-math.exp(-xsum))\n    else:\n        adj = xsum - (math.log(pair_search_space)\n                      + 2.0 * (num - 1) * math.log(starting_points))\n        adj -= _ln_gamma_int(num)\n        p = sum_pvalue(num, adj)\n    if p <= 0.0:\n        e = 0.0\n    elif p >= 1.0:\n        e = math.inf\n    else:\n        e = -math.log1p(-p) * (search_space / pair_search_space)\n\n    return {\n        \"S_prime\": s_prime,\n        \"sum_bit_score\": (stats.Lambda * s_prime - stats.logK) / math.log(2.0),\n        \"xsum\": xsum,\n        \"num_hsps\": num,\n        \"p_value\": p,\n        \"evalue\": e,\n    }\n```\n\nRun it directly or import it:\n\n```bash\npython3 -c \"import karlin_altschul as ka; print(ka.ungapped_karlin.__doc__)\"\n```\n\nA self-contained end-to-end test (embeds BLOSUM62 + Robinson frequencies) is `~/verify_solution.py`:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"End-to-end verification of the karlin_altschul module.\n\nSelf-contained: embeds the BLOSUM62 half-bit matrix and the Robinson &\nRobinson background frequencies.  Run with:  python3 verify_solution.py\n\"\"\"\nimport math\nimport sys\n\nsys.path.insert(0, \"~\")\nimport karlin_altschul as ka\n\nLETTERS = \"ARNDCQEGHILKMFPSTWYV\"\n_ROWS = \"\"\"4 -1 -2 -2 0 -1 -1 0 -2 -1 -1 -1 -1 -2 -1 1 0 -3 -2 0\n-1 5 0 -2 -3 1 0 -2 0 -3 -2 2 -1 -3 -2 -1 -1 -3 -2 -3\n-2 0 6 1 -3 0 0 0 1 -3 -3 0 -2 -3 -2 1 0 -4 -2 -3\n-2 -2 1 6 -3 0 2 -1 -1 -3 -4 -1 -3 -3 -1 0 -1 -4 -3 -3\n0 -3 -3 -3 9 -3 -4 -3 -3 -1 -1 -3 -1 -2 -3 -1 -1 -2 -2 -1\n-1 1 0 0 -3 5 2 -2 0 -3 -2 1 0 -3 -1 0 -1 -2 -1 -2\n-1 0 0 2 -4 2 5 -2 0 -3 -3 1 -2 -3 -1 0 -1 -3 -2 -2\n0 -2 0 -1 -3 -2 -2 6 -2 -4 -4 -2 -3 -3 -2 0 -2 -2 -3 -3\n-2 0 1 -1 -3 0 0 -2 8 -3 -3 -1 -2 -1 -2 -1 -2 -2 2 -3\n-1 -3 -3 -3 -1 -3 -3 -4 -3 4 2 -3 1 0 -3 -2 -1 -3 -1 3\n-1 -2 -3 -4 -1 -2 -3 -4 -3 2 4 -2 2 0 -3 -2 -1 -2 -1 1\n-1 2 0 -1 -3 1 1 -2 -1 -3 -2 5 -1 -3 -1 0 -1 -3 -2 -2\n-1 -1 -2 -3 -1 0 -2 -3 -2 1 2 -1 5 0 -2 -1 -1 -1 -1 1\n-2 -3 -3 -3 -2 -3 -3 -3 -1 0 0 -3 0 6 -4 -2 -2 1 3 -1\n-1 -2 -2 -1 -3