+<h2>Summary</h2>
+<p><strong>Root cause:</strong> The QA launcher only classified a launch dir as "git toplevel" (grade it) or "subdirectory of a repo" (refuse). A stand-in lane is a git subdirectory of a foreign parent meta-repo, so the launcher either synced/graded the parent (wrong product) or died on <code>is a SUBDIRECTORY of git repo &lt;parent&gt;</code>. It never consulted the stand-in's <code>&lt;dir&gt;/.coding-hermes/board</code> symlink, which points into the real product repo.</p>
+<p><strong>Fix:</strong> A shared <code>bunker_qa_resolve_repo_root()</code> with strict precedence:
+1. <code>BUNKER_QA_SUBDIR</code> explicit override (absolute or relative) — always wins.
+2. Stand-in board-symlink resolution: <code>readlink -f &lt;dir&gt;/.coding-hermes/board</code> → target dir's <code>git rev-parse --show-toplevel</code> → require <code>product_top != parent_top</code>, <code>product_top != launch_dir</code>, and exact round-trip; emit a loud <code>NOTE</code> naming launch dir, product root and symlink target.
+3. Named refusal — unchanged original guard, reached only when neither applies (same-parent, non-repo, broken/self symlinks, plain subdirs).</p>
+<p>Plus a read-only <code>bunker-qa resolve &lt;repo-dir&gt;</code> subcommand that prints only the decision, no agent spawn.</p>
+<p><strong>Verification (actually run):</strong></p>
+<pre><code>TOTAL: 36  PASS: 36  FAIL: 0
+</code></pre>
+<p>Covers positive resolution, override precedence/normalization/validation, and all negatives: same-parent symlink, non-repo target, broken symlink, self-target, plain-subdir refusal with the single FAIL evidence row, and a <code>$PATH</code> <code>bunker</code> stub proving <code>resolve</code> spawns no agent. The solution doc includes the full drop-in library, integration patch points, the complete test runner, and live read-only probe usage.</p></div><div class='evidence'><h2>Evidence &amp; signatures</h2><pre># Evidence
